The slopes match the regression run by hand above. The signature has no
argument for returns, by design, so the Euler equation stays a test
rather than becoming a target.

For steps 3 and 4, hand each measured loading to the pricer and compare
the rows. Everything except the loading is held at the package defaults,
so the only difference between the rows is the measured cash-flow
exposure:

::: {#795473f0 .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
leg_rows = []
for leg, claim in (("value", "Value"), ("growth", "Growth")):
    out = lrr.price_from_loadings(phi[claim])
    leg_rows.append({"leg": leg, "phi": out["loading"], "a1": out["A1"],
                     "prem": out["premium_lr"] * 100})

spread = (lrr.price_from_loadings(phi["Value"])["premium_lr"]
          - lrr.price_from_loadings(phi["Growth"])["premium_lr"])
print(f"Value-growth spread from long-run risks: {spread * 100:.2f} points")

(
    GT(pd.DataFrame(leg_rows))
    .tab_header(title="The two legs priced from measured loadings")
    .cols_label(leg="", phi="phi", a1="A1", prem="premium_lr %")
    .fmt_number(columns="phi", decimals=2)
    .fmt_number(columns="a1", decimals=1)
    .fmt_number(columns="prem", decimals=2)
    .tab_options(**KIKU_GT)
)
```

